# Lab 03: Auditd Process Spawn Monitoring

## Executive Summary
In this lab, process spawning monitoring was configured and deployed to provide enhanced visibility into suspicious commands and unauthorized processes on an endpoint. This centralized visibility was achieved by leveraging ``auditd`` for granular local logging, which was then collected and shipped via the Wazuh agent to the Wazuh manager for security analysis and alerting. The added value of this implementation is a high-fidelity telemetry stream that can be reference and analyze during passive monitoring and incident response investigations.






## MITRE ATT&CK Mapping
* **Tactics:** Priviledge Escilation ([TA0004](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0004))
* **Technique:** Create or Modify System Process ([T1543](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1543))
* **Sub-Technique:** 	Detection of Systemd Service Creation or Modification on Linux ([T1543.002](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1543/002))




## Key Take-Aways
* **System Wide Visibility:** Implementing a custom kernel-level auditd rule allowed the entire operating system on the endpoint to be monitored for process creations. Establishing this comprehensive telemetry foundation prevents visibility gaps as the lab environment scales.

* **The Noise Reduction Hypothesis:** : Logging every process spawn generates a massive volume of safe, routine system noise. My current hypothesis is that creating a blocklist (exclusion rules) for known, safe system processes will be critical for SIEM tuning, allowing the security platform to surface high-fidelity alerts without drowning in background noise.
